Subject: [PATCH] Chapter Ten source pass: correct four claims, log
 Contradictions 45-48

Checked the week of 9-13 August 1883 against named sources rather than
against the project's own notes. Four claims in the proposed Chapter Ten
skeleton did not survive it.

- 45. Post-mortems dated: Thursday 9 August, 3.30 p.m. for all five and
  4.30 p.m. for the second infant (Gould's coroner deposition, manuscript
  image 0039). Stratford hearing that same morning (Pall Mall Gazette,
  9 August). The "9th or 10th" open question is resolved.
- 46. The Daily Chronicle (13 Aug) and Dover Express (17 Aug) are one
  text, not two sources. Strengthens Contradiction 44: the Express's
  "resumed the following morning" is a compiler's bridge that, read
  literally, dates the inquest to Sunday 12 August.
- 47. The view of the house was made by the coroner "with some of the
  jurymen" (Daily Telegraph, 11 Aug), not twelve men. Corrected in the
  Elizabeth thread file, which carried it twice.
- 48. The grave was filled in at three o'clock and covered with flowers
  after that, so it was not flower-covered when the jury sat down.

Also established and not previously recorded: the jury viewed the bodies
in the mortuary before any evidence; the coroner's charge to disregard
anything heard or read; the burial orders made out before the view; the
"she wished them dead" evidence elicited by the jury, two days before a
juryman was at Elizabeth's bedside; two brothers visiting the cells on
the Saturday morning (IPN, press-tier).

Propagated corrections: the "10-foot grave" stated as fact in the
timeline file while Contradiction 42 called it irreconcilable; the
supposed Morning Post misprint "Robson", which was our transcription
error, not an 1883 press error; "flowers and descriptions" for "flowers
of all descriptions"; and the Elizabeth thread's open question on relief
money, already answered in dramatis_personae.md.

